Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:27:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 32/71] Measure the retention horizon in requests per window

The horizon a retention decision amortises over is a count of requests, not
of tokens: a kept token is re-billed once per request that follows it. It was
derived from a compiled 2K-per-turn growth guess, which puts 40 requests in a
window. Five real sessions ran at 345-850 tokens per request, so a window
actually buys around 160. Understating the horizon by four makes keeping
reasoning look four times cheaper than it is, and biases every verdict the
same way for a whole class of user.

Measure it instead. Each time a window's worth of context has been consumed,
the number of requests it took is appended to a short history in the Codex
home, and the mean of the last twenty windows is what the next session prices
against. The average is read once at session start and held fixed, so a
sample written mid-session cannot move a horizon that earlier decisions in
the same prefix were already frozen against.

This replaces the in-session growth mean, which was thrown away when the
session ended and so never accumulated enough to correct the guess.
